{:edb/db-before-t 1913 :edb/db-after-t 1914 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535226 50 #inst "2026-10-13T05:47:32.096Z" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186063960 1071 "Factor $x^2 + 12x + 36$." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186063960 1071 "Factor $x^2 + 12x + 36.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186063960 1074 "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}} x + a^2 =  (x+{\\color{blue}{a}})^2 .\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\nx^2 + 12x + 36.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+12x+{6}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with ${\\color{blue}{a}} = {\\color{blue}{6}}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+6)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186063960 1074 "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}} x + a^2 = (x+{\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\nx^2 + 12x + 36\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\nx^{2} + 12x + 6^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\nx^{2} + 2 \\cdot 6x + 6^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{a}} = {\\color{blue}{6}}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n(x + 6)^{2}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186063961 1071 "Factor $p^2 - 10p + 25$." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186063961 1071 "Factor $p^2 - 10p + 25.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186063961 1074 "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\np^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}} p + a^2 =  (p-{\\color{blue}{a}})^2 .\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\np^2 - 10p + 25.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}-10p+{5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}-2\\cdot 5p+{5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with ${\\color{blue}{a}} = {\\color{blue}{5}}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186063961 1074 "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\np^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}} p + a^2 = (p-{\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\np^2 - 10p + 25\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\np^{2} - 10p + 5^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\np^{2} - 2 \\cdot 5p + 5^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{a}} = {\\color{blue}{5}}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n(p - 5)^{2}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186063962 1071 "Factor the expression $y^2 + \\dfrac{2}{5}y + \\dfrac{1}{25}$." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186063962 1071 "Factor the expression $y^2 + \\dfrac{2}{5}y + \\dfrac{1}{25}.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186063962 1074 "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}} x + a^2 =  (x+{\\color{blue}{a}})^2 .\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\ny^2 + \\dfrac{2}{5}y + \\dfrac{1}{25}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}y+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with ${\\color{blue}{a}} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 1 5}}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(y+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186063962 1074 "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}} x + a^2 = (x+{\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\ny^2 + \\dfrac{2}{5}y + \\dfrac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\ny^{2} + \\frac{2}{5}y + (\\frac{1}{5})^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\ny^{2} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y + (\\frac{1}{5})^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{a}} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 1 5}}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n(y + \\frac{1}{5})^{2}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186063991 1071 "Expand $(4+3y)^2$." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186063991 1071 "Expand $(4+3y)^2.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186063991 1074 "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=4$ and $b=3y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (4 + 3y)^2 \\\\ &= (4)^2 + 2(4) (3y) + (3y)^2 \\\\ &= 16 + 24y + 9y^2. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186063991 1074 "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=4$ and $b=3y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(4 + 3y)^2 &= \\\n\t\t(4)^2 + 2(4) ( 3y) + (3y)^2 &= \\\n\t\t16 + 24y + 9y^2.\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186063992 1071 "Expand $(2n^2 + 3)^2$." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186063992 1071 "Expand $(2n^2 + 3)^2.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186063992 1074 "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=2n^2$ and $b=3$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (2n^2 + 3)^2 \\\\[2pt] &= (2n^2)^2 + 2(2n^2) (3) + (3)^2 \\\\[2pt] &= 2^2\\cdot (n^2)^2 + 2(2n^2) (3) + (3)^2 \\\\[2pt] &= 4n^4 + 12n^2 + 9. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186063992 1074 "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=2n^2$ and $b=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(2n^2 + 3)^2 &= \\[2pt]\n\t\t(2n^2)^2 + 2(2n^2) ( 3) + (3)^2 &= \\[2pt]\n\t\t2^2\\cdot (n^2)^2 + 2(2n^2) (3) + (3)^2 &= \\[2pt]\n\t\t4n^4 + 12n^2 + 9.\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186063993 1071 "Expand $(3p - 5)^2$." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186063993 1071 "Expand $(3p - 5)^2.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186063993 1074 "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a-b)^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=3p$ and $b=5$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (3p - 5)^2 \\\\ &= (3p)^2 - 2(3p)(5) + 5^2 \\\\ &= 9p^2 - 30p + 25. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186063993 1074 "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a-b)^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=3p$ and $b=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(3p - 5)^2 &= \\\n\t\t(3p)^2 - 2(3p)(5) + 5^2 &= \\\n\t\t9p^2 - 30p + 25.\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186064232 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482538 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186064232 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482578 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186064233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482519 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186064233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482545 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186064233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482593 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186064233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482604 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186064234 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482554 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186064234 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482594 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186064325 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482518 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186064325 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482579 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186064326 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482527 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186064326 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482567 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186064326 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482612 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186064327 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482523 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186064327 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482529 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186064441 1050 420.0 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186064447 1050 420.0 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186065341 1064 "A **perfect square trinomial** is a three-term expression that is the result of squaring a binomial.\n\nFor example, the expression\n\n$$\nx^2 + 6x + 9\n$$\n\nis a perfect square trinomial because it is the result of squaring the binomial $(x+3)$, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor a perfect square trinomial, we have to figure out what binomial it is the square of. We can often do this by relating the perfect square trinomial to the **square of a sum**:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}2}  {\\color{blue}a}x + {\\color{blue}a}^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}a})^2\n$$\n\n \n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $x^2+6x+9$ using the square of sum formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Factor the trinomial using the \"square of a sum formula\". In this case, we have ${\\color{blue}{a}}={\\color{blue}{3}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\nThis method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial. We will learn more general methods in the future." 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186065341 1064 "A **perfect square trinomial** is a three-term expression that is the result of squaring a binomial.\n\nFor example, the expression\n$x^2 + 6x + 9$\nis a perfect square trinomial because it is the result of squaring the binomial $(x+3)$, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 3)^{2} &=  \\\\\nx^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3x + 3^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 6x + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor a perfect square trinomial, we have to figure out what binomial it is the square of. We can often do this by relating the perfect square trinomial to the **square of a sum**:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}2} {\\color{blue}a}x + {\\color{blue}a}^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}a})^2\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $x^2+6x+9$ using the square of sum formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1. Write the constant term as a perfect square:\n$x^{2} + 6x + 3^{2}$\n2. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n$x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3x + 3^{2}$\n3. Factor the trinomial using the \"square of a sum formula\". In this case, we have ${\\color{blue}{a}}={\\color{blue}{3}}$:\n$(x + 3)^{2}$\n\nThis method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial. We will learn more general methods in the future." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186065345 1064 "Other perfect square trinomials can be factored as the **square of a difference**:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}2}  {\\color{blue}a}x  + {\\color{blue}a}^2 = (x - {\\color{blue}a})^2\n$$\n\n \n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $x^2-10x+25$ using the square of difference formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x+{5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula. In this case, we have ${\\color{blue}{a}}={\\color{blue}{5}},$ and we get\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n  $$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186065345 1064 "Other perfect square trinomials can be factored as the **square of a difference**:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}2} {\\color{blue}a}x + {\\color{blue}a}^2 = (x - {\\color{blue}a})^2\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $x^2-10x+25$ using the square of difference formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1. Write the constant term as a perfect square:\n$x^{2} - 10x + 5^{2}$\n2. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n$x^{2} - 2 \\cdot 5x + 5^{2}$\n3. Factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula. In this case, we have ${\\color{blue}{a}}={\\color{blue}{5}}$, and we get\n$(x - 5)^{2}$." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186065441 1064 "When we want to calculate the **square of a binomial**, like\n\n$$\n(a+b)^2\n$$\n\nthere's a formula that we can use instead of working out the product $(a+b)(a+b)$ manually. The formula is\n\n$$\n(a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2\n$$\n\nIt might be helpful to remember this as *\"the square of the first term, plus the square of the second term, plus twice the product.\"*\n\nSuppose we need to calculate the square $(x+3)^2$. In this case, we have $a= x$ and $b=3$. We substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 3)^{2} &=  \\\\\n(x)^{2} + 2(x)(3) + (3)^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 6x + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula gives the correct result by working out the multiplication manually:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (x + 3)^2 \\\\[3pt] &= (x + 3)(x + 3) \\\\[3pt] &= x(x + 3) + 3(x + 3) \\\\[3pt] &= x^2 + 3x + 3x + 9 \\\\[3pt] &= x^2 + 6x + 9 &\\, {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the same result! But by using the formula, we save a lot of time and effort." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186065441 1064 "When we want to calculate the **square of a binomial**, like\n\n$$\n(a+b)^2,\n$$\n\nthere's a formula that we can use instead of working out the product $(a+b)(a+b)$ manually. The formula is\n\n$$\n(a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2.\n$$\n\nIt might be helpful to remember this as *\"the square of the first term, plus the square of the second term, plus twice the product.\"*\n\nSuppose we need to calculate the square $(x+3)^2.$ In this case, we have $a= x$ and $b=3.$ We substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & = \\\\ (x)^{2}+2(x)(3)+(3)^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula gives the correct result by working out the multiplication manually:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(x + 3)^2 &= \\[3pt]\n\t\t(x + 3)(x + 3) &= \\[3pt]\n\t\tx(x + 3) + 3(x + 3) &= \\[3pt]\n\t\tx^2 + 3x + 3x + 9 &= \\[3pt]\n\t\tx^2 + 6x + 9 &\\, {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n\t}\n$$\n\nSo we get the same result! But by using the formula, we save a lot of time and effort." 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186065442 1064 "So far, we've been using the formula for the square of a binomial involving addition:\n\n$$\n(a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2\n$$\n\nThere is also a similar formula that we can use to compute the square of a binomial involving subtraction. The formula is\n\n$$\n(a - b)^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2\n$$\n\nFor example, to compute the square $(x-3)^2$, we can substitute $a=x$ and $b=3$ into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 3)^{2} &=  \\\\\n(x)^{2} - 2(x)(3) + (3)^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 6x + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula gives the correct result by working out the multiplication manually:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (x - 3)^2 \\\\ &= (x - 3)(x - 3) \\\\ &= x(x - 3) - 3(x - 3) \\\\ &= x^2 - 3x - 3x + 9 \\\\ &= x^2 - 6x + 9 &\\, {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we get the same result." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186065442 1064 "So far, we've been using the formula for the square of a binomial involving addition:\n\n$$\n(a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2\n$$\n\nThere is also a similar formula that we can use to compute the square of a binomial involving subtraction. The formula is\n\n$$\n(a - b)^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2.\n$$\n\nFor example, to compute the square $(x-3)^2,$ we can substitute $a=x$ and $b=3$ into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & = \\\\ (x)^{2}-2(x)(3)+(3)^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+9 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check that the formula gives the correct result by working out the multiplication manually:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(x - 3)^2 &= \\\n\t\t(x - 3)(x - 3) &= \\\n\t\tx(x - 3) - 3(x - 3) &= \\\n\t\tx^2 - 3x - 3x + 9 &= \\\n\t\tx^2 - 6x + 9 &\\, {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n\t}\n$$\n\nAgain, we get the same result." 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186066112 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482290 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186066112 1078 #edb/ref 17592186482290 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186068486 1071 "If $g(x) = (x-4)^2+3(5-x)$, calculate $g(2)$." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186068486 1071 "If $g(x) = (x-4)^2+3(5-x)$, calculate $g(2).$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186068486 1074 "We substitute $x=2$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} g(x) &= (x-4)^2+3(5-x) \\\\[5pt] g(2) &= (2-4)^2+3(5-2) \\\\[5pt] &= (-2)^2+3(3) \\\\[5pt] &= 4 + 9 \\\\[5pt] &= 13 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186068486 1074 "We substitute $x=2$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tg(x) &= (x-4)^2+3(5-x) \\[5pt]\n\t\tg(2) &= (2-4)^2+3(5-2) \\[5pt]\n\t\t      &= (-2)^2+3(3) \\[5pt]\n\t\t      &= 4 + 9 \\[5pt]\n\t\t      &= 13\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186068487 1071 "Evaluate $f(4,-1)$ if $f(x,y)= 3\\cdot 2^{x+3y}$." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186068487 1071 "Evaluate $f(4,-1)$ if $f(x,y)= 3\\cdot 2^{x+3y}.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186068487 1074 "We substitute $x=4$ and $y=-1$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y) &= 3 \\cdot 2^{x + 3y} \\\\\nf(4,-1) &= 3 \\cdot 2^{4 + 3(-1)} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 2^{4 - 3} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 2^{1} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186068487 1074 "We substitute $x=4$ and $y=-1$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =3\\cdot {2}^{x+3y} \\\\ f(4,-1) & =3\\cdot {2}^{4+3(-1)} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{4-3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186068701 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482561 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186068701 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482574 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186068702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482526 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186068702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482569 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186068995 1050 420.0 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186069116 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186069116 1071 "If $g(a, b) = (a - b)(a^{2} - b^{2})$, then $g(2,-2) =$ {{field-1}}." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186069116 1071 "If $g(a,b)=(a-b)({a}^{2}-{b}^{2}),$ then $g(2,-2)=${{field-1}}." 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186069116 1074 "We substitute $a=2$ and $b=-2$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(a,b) & =(a-b)({a}^{2}-{b}^{2}) \\\\ g(2,-2) & =((2)-(-2))((2)^{2}-(-2)^{2}) \\\\  & =(2+2)(4-4) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186070022 1064 "A **function** is a rule that takes a number as **input** and then **outputs** another number.\n\nFor instance, let's look at the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 3x+1\n$$\n\nIn this example,\n\n- $f$ is the **name** of the function,\n- the number 