{2}} & = \\\\ 8 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186078319 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\sqrt{((5^2-4) \\div 3)-2}$." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186078319 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\sqrt{((5^2-4) \\div 3)-2}.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186078319 1074 "To simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations,\n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses, and\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents.\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(({5}^{2}-4)÷3)-2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{((25-4)÷3)-2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(21÷3)-2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{7-2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186078319 1074 "To simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations,\n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses, and\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents.\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{((5^{2} - 4) \\div 3) - 2} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{((25 - 4) \\div 3) - 2} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{(21 \\div 3) - 2} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{7 - 2} &=  \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{5} \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186078320 1074 "To simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations,\n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses, and\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents.\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{13\\times 2-5}+1 & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{26-5}+1 & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{21}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we approximate the value of the radical using a calculator:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{21} \\approx 4.582\\,576\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\nFinally, we approximate the value of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{21}+1 & ≈ \\\