5485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186079185 1071 "What is the value of $\\sqrt{2 \\cdot 4 - 6}$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186079185 1071 "What is the value of $\\sqrt{2\\times 4-6}$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186079185 1074 "To simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations: \n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2\\times 4-6} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{8-6} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we approximate the value of $\\sqrt{2}$ using a calculator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & ≈‭‭‭1.414\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186079665 1064 "Suppose we want to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n-3\\sqrt{\\left(2^4-1\\right) \\div 3}\\,\n$$\n\nThe first thing to realize is that there is an invisible multiplication symbol here. The above expression is short for\n\n$$\n-3\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}\\,\\sqrt{\\left(2^4-1\\right) \\div 3}\\,\n$$\n\nWhen multiplying a radical by a number, we usually omit the additional multiplication symbol.\n\nNow, to simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations:\n\n- Expressions underneath radicals are treated as if they are contained within parentheses (the **P** in **P**EMDAS), so we always deal with this first.\n- Radicals themselves are treated as exponents (the **E** in P**E**MDAS).\n\nTherefore, following PEMDAS, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3\\sqrt{(2^{4} - 1) \\div 3} &=  \\\\\n- 3\\sqrt{(16 - 1) \\div 3} &=  \\\\\n- 3\\sqrt{15 \\div 3} &=  \\\\\n&= - 3\\sqrt{5} \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's our answer!\n\nTo get a numerical approximation, we need to use a calculator. In this case, we get\n\n$$\n-3 \\sqrt{5} = -3\\times \\sqrt{5} \\approx -6.708\\,204\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186079665 1064 "Suppose we want to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n-3\\sqrt{\\left(2^4-1\\right) \\div 3}\\,.\n$$\n\nThe first thing to realize is that there is an invisible multiplication symbol here. The above expression is short for\n\n$$\n-3\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}\\,\\sqrt{\\left(2^4-1\\right) \\div 3}\\,.\n$$\n\nWhen multiplying a radical by a number, we usually omit the additional multiplication symbol.\n\nNow, to simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS.  In terms of the order of operations:\n\n- Expressions underneath radicals are treated as if they are contained within parentheses (the **P** in **P**EMDAS), so we always deal with this first.\n\n- Radicals themselves are treated as exponents (the **E** in P**E**MDAS).\n\nTherefore, following PEMDAS, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\sqrt{({2}^{4}-1)÷3} & = \\\\ -3\\sqrt{(16-1)÷3} & = \\\\ -3\\sqrt{15÷3} & = \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's our answer! \n\nTo get a numerical approximation, we need to use a calculator. In this case, we get\n\n$$\n-3 \\sqrt{5} = -3\\times \\sqrt{5} \\approx -6.708\\,204\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places." 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186079667 1064 "Suppose we need to evaluate an expression like\n\n$$\n1+\\sqrt{4},\n$$\n\nwhere we have a square root sign. What would be the order of operations? It turns out that the root signs ($\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$ or $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}$) can be thought of as parentheses. \nIn other words, we find the value under the square root first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\sqrt{4} & = \\\\ 1+2 & = \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSimilarly, let's evaluate $\\big(1-\\sqrt[3]{-27}\\big) \\div 2$.\n\nTo evaluate expressions with radicals, we use PEMDAS as before, but with the following additional rules:\n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nFollowing the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-\\sqrt[3]{-27})÷2 & = \\\\ (1-\\sqrt[3]{(-3)^{3}})÷2 & = \\\\ (1-(-3))÷2 & = \\\\ 4÷2 & = \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186079667 1064 "Suppose we need to evaluate an expression like\n$1+\\sqrt{4}$,\nwhere we have a square root sign. What would be the order of operations? It turns out that the root signs ($\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$ or $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}$) can be thought of as parentheses.\nIn other words, we find the value under the square root first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + \\sqrt{4} &=  \\\\\n1 + 2 &=  \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, let's evaluate $\\big(1-\\sqrt[3]{-27}\\big) \\div 2$.\n\nTo evaluate expressions with radicals, we use PEMDAS as before, but with the following additional rules:\n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nFollowing the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1 - \\sqrt[3]{-27}) \\div 2 &=  \\\\\n(1 - \\sqrt[3]{(-3)^{3}}) \\div 2 &=  \\\\\n(1 - (-3)) \\div 2 &=  \\\\\n4 \\div 2 &=  \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186124455 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482608 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186126643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126644 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186126643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126645 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186126643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126646 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186126643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126647 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186126643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126648 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186126643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481799 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186126643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482209 13194139535226 1759218