ing triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186129524 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/d4/d497c83f7eb77b0b0950f5a1c1f5d429574b2734743ef91bbdb57541f7f7d90f.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}{\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186129758 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186129758 1071 "![](images/22/2206a1bc02acb9ddccc5991a4c8d36c9e5d3255e72239e8f7e81908e10faab48.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 18y$ for $x \\ge 0$ and $z \\ge 0$, shown above.\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates.\n$∭_(R)xydV$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186129758 1071 "![](images/22/2206a1bc02acb9ddccc5991a4c8d36c9e5d3255e72239e8f7e81908e10faab48.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=18y$ for $x\\ge 0$ and $z\\ge 0,$ shown above. \nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}xy\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186129758 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $3$ centered at $(0,3,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=18y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =18y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =18\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -18\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =18\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/57/578c7f81a0dae8339772dec9a9407fb380866e5dde5fdd18fee04feb6afe5749.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 18\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=xy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xy\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{18\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186129761 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186129761 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186129761 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral\n$∭_(R)\\frac{2}{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}dV$\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 1$." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186129761 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{\\rho }^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{2}{{\\rho }^{2}}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}d\\rho  \\\\  & =2[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot [\\rho ]_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =1} \\\\  & =-2(-1-1)\\cdot (2\\pi -0)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186130455 1064 "When evaluating triple integrals, it's sometimes easier to change the variables from Cartesian coordinates to spherical polar coordinates.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R \\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2\\right)^{3/2} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the region enclosed by the sphere of radius $1$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/08/0809a74f0aca2b3042b7f8db6783f9d6a13bc84bfc75ddcbb4d3076dbf0a2efd.png)\n\nThis integral seems too challenging when written in Cartesian coordinates. However, we should recognize that the problem lends itself to spherical polar coordinates because\n\n- the domain $R$ has a simple spherical polar representation, and\n\n- the integrand $f(x,y,z)=\\left(x^2+y^2+z^2\\right)^{3/2}$ can also be expressed simply in spherical polar coordinates.\n\nSo, let's transform the entire problem to spherical polar coordinates using the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined by\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\n\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \n\nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi.\n$$\n\nThe preimage of $R$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the rectangular region $\\Delta,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi, \\: 0 \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/c2/c2ed8a57c5e66687428a524eb148f96672f3a1ace7fbd28d426de065b2fb5f1f.png)\n\nTo express our integral in terms of spherical polar coordinates, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(\\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\cos\\phi) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x,y,z)}{\\partial (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)} \\right| \\: \\mathrm{d}\\rho \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta \\, \\mathrm{d}\\phi.\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant for a spherical change of variables is\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x,y,z)}{\\partial (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)} \\right| = \\rho^2\\sin\\phi.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change of variables formula is\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(\\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\cos\\phi) \\:  \\rho^2\\sin\\phi \\: \\mathrm{d}\\rho \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta \\, \\mathrm{d}\\phi.\n$$\n\nNow, let's write our function $f(x,y,z) = \\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2\\right)^{3/2}$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & ={({\\rho }^{2})}^{3/2} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{3}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{6}{\\rho }^{6}]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =1}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =-(-1-1)\\cdot (2\\pi -0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}(1-0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186130455 1064 "When evaluating triple integrals, it's sometimes easier to change the variables from Cartesian coordinates to spherical polar coordinates.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to evaluate t