86045417 true] [17592186162002 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482610 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186162003 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482506 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186162003 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482524 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186162003 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482592 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186162003 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482617 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186162004 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482542 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186162004 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482550 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186162004 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482565 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186162004 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482588 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186162004 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482596 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186162178 1050 840.0 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186162179 1050 420.0 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186162641 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186162641 1071 "Find the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1, \\\\ 1-{e}^{1-x},\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186162641 1071 "Find the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$F(x) = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1, \\\\ 1 - e^{1 - x},\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 1$." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186162641 1074 "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-{e}^{1-{q}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {e}^{1-{q}_{1}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1-{q}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\ {q}_{1} & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1-{e}^{1-{q}_{3}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {e}^{1-{q}_{3}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-{q}_{3} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ {q}_{3} & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}IQR & ={q}_{3}-{q}_{1} \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{4})-(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186162644 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186162644 1071 "Find the $30$th percentile of the random variable $Y$ if the probability distribution function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y},\\, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186162644 1071 "Find the $30$th percentile of the random variable $Y$ if the probability distribution function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$f(y) = {e^{-y},\\begin{vmatrix}y \\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186162644 1074 "The $100p$th percentile of a random variable $Y$ is the number ${y}_{p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le {y}_{p})=p.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we want to find the $30$th percentile, which corresponds to the decimal $p=0.3.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le {y}_{0.3}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{{y}_{0.3}}{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{e}^{-y}{|}_{0}^{{y}_{0.3}} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-{y}_{0.3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $30$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-{y}_{0.3}} & =0.3 \\\\ {e}^{-{y}_{0.3}} & =0.7 \\\\ {e}^{-{y}_{0.3}} & =\\frac{7}{10} \\\\ -{y}_{0.3} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{10}) \\\\ {y}_{0.3} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{7}) \\\\ {y}_{0.3} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186162651 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186162651 1071 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X$?\n\n$f(x) = {\\frac{15}{128}(4x^{2} - x^{4}),\\begin{vmatrix}-2 \\le x \\le 2 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186162651 1071 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15}{128}(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}), & -2\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186162651 1074 "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-2\\le x\\le 2$ is $f(x)=\\frac{15}{128}(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{15}{128}(8x-4{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{32}(2x-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{32}x(2-{x}^{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{32}x(2-{x}^{2})=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[-2,2],$ so we consider them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=\\pm 2.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,\\pm \\sqrt{2},\\pm 2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =0 \\\\ f(-\\sqrt{2}) & =\\frac{15}{128}(4{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}-{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{4})=\\frac{15}{128}(8-4)=\\frac{15}{32} \\\\ f(0) & =\\frac{15}{128}(4{(0)}^{2}-{(0)}^{4})=0 \\\\ f(\\sqrt{2}) & =\\frac{15}{128}(4{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{2})}^{4})=\\frac{15}{128}(8-4)=\\frac{15}{32} \\\\ f(2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-\\sqrt{2})=f(\\sqrt{2})=\\frac{15}{32}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186163239 1064 "If $X$ is a continuous random variable and\n\n$$\n0 \\leq p \\leq 1\n$$\n\nthen the $100p$th **percentile** is a number $x_p$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq x_p) = p\n$$\n\n- In terms of CDF, the *cumulative distribution function* $F(x)$ of $X$, the $100p$th percentile $x_p$ is a number on the $x$-axis such that\n\n$$\nF(x_p) = p\n$$\n\n- In terms of PDF, the *probability density function* $f(x)$ of $X$, the $100p$th percentile $x_p$ is a number on the $x$-axis such that the area under the graph of $y=f(x)$ to the left of $x$ is equal to $p$:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{x_p} f(x) \\, \\text{d}x = p\n$$\n\nFor example, let's find the $4$th percentile of the random variable $X$ if the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{2}x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nThe $4$th percentile corresponds to the decimal\n\n$$\np={\\color{red}0.04}\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n100 \\cdot 0.04 = 4\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le x_{0.04}) &= \\int_{0}^{x_{0.04}}\\frac{1}{2}xdx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{x^{2}}{4}\\right|_{0}^{x_{0.04}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{x_{0.04}^{2}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $4$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x_{0.04}^{2}}{4} &= 0.04 \\\\\nx_{0.04}^{2} &= 0.16 \\\\\nx_{0.04} &= 0.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x_{0.04} \\leq 2\n$$\n\nThere are a few special percentiles:\n\n- the $25$th percentile is called the **lower quartile**. This is the number $q_1$ such that\n$P(X \\leq q_1) = 0.25$.\n- the $50$th percentile is called the **median**. This is the number $m$ such that\n$P(X \\leq m) = 0.5$.\n- the $75$th percentile is called the **upper quartile**. This is the number $q_3$ such that\n$P(X \\leq q_3) = 0.75$.\n- the **interquartile range** is the difference between the upper and lower quartiles, that is,\n$IQR = q_3 - q_1$." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186163239 1064 "If $X$ is a continuous random variable and $0 \\leq p \\leq 1,$ then the $100p$th **percentile** is a number $x_p$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq x_p) = p.\n$$\n\n- In terms of CDF, the *cumulative distribution function* $F(x)$ of $X,$ the $100p$th percentile $x_p$ is a number on the $x$-axis such that\n\n$$\nF(x_p) = p.\n$$\n\n- In terms of PDF, the *probability density function* $f(x)$ of $X,$ the $100p$th percentile $x_p$ is a number on the $x$-axis such that the area under the graph of $y=f(x)$ to the left of $x$ is equal to $p \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{x_p} f(x) \\, \\text{d}x = p.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's find the $4$th percentile of the random variable $X$ if the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n![](images/69/69a9f969e3caaa3cc3d896b5a8b6ed566cff3517743519f0c1088e588c3967e7.svg)\n\nThe $4$th percentile corresponds to the decimal $p={\\color{red}0.04}$ since $100 \\cdot 0.04 = 4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {x}_{0.04}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{{x}_{0.04}}\\frac{1}{2}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}|}_{0}^{{x}_{0.04}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{{x}_{0.04}}^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $4$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{{x}_{0.04}}^{2}}{4} & =0.04 \\\\ {{x}_{0.04}}^{2} & =0.16 \\\\ {x}_{0.04} & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $0 \\leq x_{0.04} \\leq 2.$\n\nThere are a few special percentiles:\n\n- the $25$th percentile is called the **lower quartile**. This is the number $q_1$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq q_1) = 0.25.\n$$\n\n- the $50$th percentile is called the **median**. This is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq m) = 0.5.\n$$\n\n- the $75$th percentile is called the **upper quartile**. This is the number $q_3$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq q_3) = 0.75.\n$$\n\n- the **interquartile range** is the difference between the upper and lower quartiles, that is,\n\n$$\nIQR = q_3 - q_1.\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186163240 1064 "The **modes of a random variable $X$** with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x)$.\n\nFor example, the modes of the random variable $X$ with the probability density function shown below are ${\\color{blue}5}$ and ${\\color{blue}9}$ (the values of $x$ that correspond to the global maxima):\n\n![](images/49/49e3f3c63c01ce5c00e9d2ff51bcd03d7516f7866f510ce091071a13a8496210.png)" 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186163240 1064 "The **modes of a random variable $X$** with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFor example, the modes of the random variable $X$ with the probability density function shown below are ${\\color{blue}5}$ and ${\\color{blue}9}$ (the values of $x$ that correspond to the global maxima):\n\n![](images/49/49e3f3c63c01ce5c00e9d2ff51bcd03d7516f7866f510ce091071a13a8496210.png)" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186163259 1064 "Now, suppose we are given the random variable $X$ with the probability density function shown below.\n\n$$\n{2x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nHow can we find the mode of $X$ algebraically?\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x)$, we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ is $f(x) =2x$. Taking the derivative, we get\n$f^{′}(x)|= 2$.\nSo, there are no solutions of $f'(x) = 0$.\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x = 0,1$. So, these are critical points.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0) &= 2(0) = 0 \\\\\nf(1) &= 2(1) = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf({\\color{blue}1}) =2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is ${\\color{blue}1}$." 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186163259 1064 "Now, suppose we are given the random variable $X$ with the probability density function shown below. \n\n![](images/ec/ec48a5d839f3d843fe4738707ec3d067a19db9b009f4bf29673d899b321248a3.svg)\n\nHow can we find the mode of $X$ algebraically? \n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ is $f(x) =2x.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions  of  $f'(x) = 0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x = 0,1.$ So, these are critical points.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =2(0)=0 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf({\\color{blue}1}) =2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is ${\\color{blue}1}.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186175042 1078 #edb/ref 17592186175047 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186175917 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482577 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186175917 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482600 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186176484 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186176484 1071 "What is $(\\sqrt{64})^{2}$?" 13194139535226 17592186045417 false] [17592186176484 1071 "What is $(\\sqrt{64})^{2}?$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186176484