592186045417 true] [17592186482502 1079 #uuid "f9264828-72bc-5feb-b279-b58d31b33327" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482503 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481804 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482503 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482059 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482503 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482216 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482503 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482245 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482503 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482339 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482503 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482503 1077 "selection" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482503 1078 #edb/ref 17592186482216 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482503 1079 #uuid "fdf80c47-03de-50d9-aa6a-97be39508d6b" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482416 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482504 1069 "q-161787" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482504 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482504 1071 "![](images/61/61bd212879bc2f37dba7637bb3b126e33c5f7b0fbebd5a676874d9b3f6bc38da.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=6y$ for $x\\ge 0,$ shown above." 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482504 1072 #uuid "0136e908-03ab-5353-9218-7031e36c58ca" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482504 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $3$ centered at $(0,3,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=6y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =6y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =6\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/f5/f52fe1c2e73dcbe972d72d263a5a43f2c99dbd573395fbf6e3a3b78b46e1f461.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}[\\rho ]_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ-0)\\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,d\\theta  \\\\  & =6[ϕ]_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =6(\\pi -0)\\cdot (-0+1) \\\\  & =6\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482378 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482505 1069 "q-94705" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482505 1071 "Find the $90$th percentile of the random variable $X$ if the probability distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482505 1072 #uuid "049efa61-2f95-573c-8d4c-3a15cf157276" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482505 1074 "The $100p$th percentile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${x}_{p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {x}_{p})=p.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we want to find the $90$th percentile, which corresponds to the decimal $p=0.9.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {x}_{0.9}) & ={\\int }_{1}^{{x}_{0.9}}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{x}_{0.9}}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{-1/2}{|}_{1}^{{x}_{0.9}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}_{0.9}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $90$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}_{0.9}}} & =0.9 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}_{0.9}}} & =0.1 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}_{0.9}} & =10 \\\\ {x}_{0.9} & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482501 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482506 1069 "q-97023" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482506 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482506 1071 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x(6-x)^{2}}{128}, & 0\\le x\\le 8 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482506 1072 #uuid "04da65b8-370f-5d43-9d9a-986d048126c3" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482506 1074 "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 8$ is $f(x)=\\frac{x(6-x)^{2}}{128}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(6-x)^{2}-2x(6-x)}{128} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6-x)(6-x-2x)}{128} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(6-x)(2-x)}{128},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3(6-x)(2-x)}{128}\\,⟹\\,x=2,6.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[0,8],$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,8.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,2,6,8:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{0(6-0)^{2}}{128}=0 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{2(6-2)^{2}}{128}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ f(6) & =\\frac{6(6-6)^{2}}{128}=0 \\\\ f(8) & =\\frac{8(6-8)^{2}}{128}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(2)=f(8)=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $2$ and $8.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482507 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482379 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482507 1069 "q-67051" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482507 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482507 1071 "What is the third term of the expression $-3k+2l-7m+\\frac{2}{3}n?$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482507 1072 #uuid "04e6642c-b46c-5677-a772-7f923b6e600b" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482507 1074 "We put an addition symbol $(+)$ between each part and separate using parentheses:\n\n$$\n(-3k)+(2l)+(-7m)+(\\frac{2}{3}n)\n$$\n\nCounting off the terms:\n\n- the first term is $-3k,$\n\n- the second term is $2l,$\n\n- the third term is $-7m,$ and\n\n- the fourth term is $\\frac{2}{3}n.$\n\nIn particular, we are interested in the third term. The third term is $-7m.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482508 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482408 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482508 1069 "q-125516" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482508 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482508 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{4}}{195}+\\frac{38}{65}, & 2<x\\le 3, \\\\ 1, & x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482508 1072 #uuid "07427b74-a9a0-5dbd-ab84-a95a81a836e0" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482508 1074 "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $F(2)=\\frac{2}{3}>\\frac{1}{2},$ we have $0\\le m\\le 2.$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{m}{3}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482403 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482405 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482490 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482509 1069 "q-336479" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482509 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482509 1071 "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(0)=c$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,x(0)$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ is obtained from the defining initial value problem for ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ by  \n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by  \n{{field-3}}." 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482509 1072 #uuid "1163c64f-8eb6-5387-bd44-2f1d21a98032" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482509 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is a fundamental matrix for the system, every solution can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c\\,\\text{for some constant vector }c.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{properties of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ The standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is defined as the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nThe second condition, ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},$ is exactly line $\\text{L4}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ is obtained from the defining initial value problem for ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ by $\\text{using the initial condition }{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L4},$ and $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c,\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L4}$ we know ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.$ Replacing ${Φ}_{0}(0)$ with ${I}_{n}$ in the equation $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c$ gives\n\n$$\nx(0)={I}_{n}c=c,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly line $\\text{L5}.$ Thus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from line $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{replacing }{Φ}_{0}(0)\\text{ with }{I}_{n}\\text{ in }x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482434 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482510 1069 "q-161758" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482510 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482510 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x+y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482510 1072 #uuid "1521c62d-edb7-5cf4-9412-1fda40e8b968" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482510 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=x+y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ+\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x+y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482496 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482511 1069 "q-39058" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482511 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482511 1071 "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le x+y\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le y+z\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dV={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482511 1072 #uuid "15e16006-8a30-5d22-b639-4bcd0e964462" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482511 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$ \n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps: \n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le u\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482498 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482512 1069 "q-161759" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482512 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=5$ for $y\\ge 0.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482512 1072 #uuid "164ec461-433f-5d34-8b46-e1e7c87d7fe4" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482512 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/9c/9ca3a428d54a15b11c4c5bd95039df6a0b2d9f4363dba7034c51edb2b6f8fd27.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{5},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}} \\\\  & =\\rho \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482471 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482513 1069 "q-69301" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482513 1071 "$\\sqrt{5}÷(1-{3}^{2})=$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482513 1072 #uuid "1c3c665d-7d8f-59dd-ac27-822edd107571" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482513 1074 "To simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations: \n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}÷(1-{3}^{2}) & = \\\\ \\sqrt{5}÷(1-9) & = \\\\ \\sqrt{5}÷(-8) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{(-8)} & = \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482427 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482514 1069 "q-40893" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482514 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482514 1071 "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482514 1072 #uuid "22478d60-239d-58ef-b70b-d84ab0dcc7e7" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482514 1074 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{198}[\\begin{aligned}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{198}[\\begin{aligned}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482491 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482515 1069 "q-161892" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482515 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482515 1071 "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 2,\\,1\\le xy\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le xy+3\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x},\\,v=xy,\\,w=z-xy,\n$$\n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}xyz\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482515 1072 #uuid "22a02818-7cda-52f6-bd03-f172a29927f5" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482515 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x},\\,v=xy,\\,w=z-xy,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 3,\\,-v\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ -y & -x & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{x}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\  & =-2u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xyz\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}v(w+v)\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{v(w+v)}{2u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-v}^{3}\\frac{v(w+v)}{2u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482423 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482516 1069 "q-125510" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482516 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482516 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{3}}{64}, & 0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482516 1072 #uuid "26759ee4-9182-5088-bdc1-26b84fc02211" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482516 1074 "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=F(m)=\\frac{{m}^{3}}{64},\\,0\\le m\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{m}^{3}}{64}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,m=\\sqrt[3]{32}.\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482517 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482458 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482517 1069 "q-94641" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482517 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482517 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $Y$ if the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6{e}^{-6y},\\, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482517 1072 #uuid "2b515338-9a49-5088-8d83-3ec16988abce" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482517 1074 "The median of a random variable $Y$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le m) & ={\\int }_{0}^{m}6{e}^{-6y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{e}^{-6y}{|}_{0}^{m} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-6m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-6m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {e}^{-6m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -6m & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ -6m & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ m & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482478 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482518 1069 "q-111601" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482518 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482518 1071 "Factor ${p}^{2}+49+14p.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482518 1072 #uuid "2bee595e-f4bd-59ff-8622-53fe58115cfe" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482518 1074 "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}+2ap+{a}^{2}=(p+a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}+49+14p={p}^{2}+14p+49.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}+14p+{7}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}+2\\cdot 7p+{7}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with $a=7.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p+7)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482381 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482497 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482519 1069 "q-216488" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482519 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482519 1071 "${(2{x}^{2}+3)}^{2}\\,\\,=\\,\\,${{field-1}}${x}^{4}\\,\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}\\,\\,+9$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482519 1072 #uuid "2d137423-8c38-5c63-aa2d-733719761261" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482519 1074 "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{2}={a}^{2}+2ab+{b}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=2{x}^{2}$ and $b=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2{x}^{2}+3)}^{2} & = \\\\ {(2{x}^{2})}^{2}+2(2{x}^{2})(3)+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ 4{x}^{4}+12{x}^{2}+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482520 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482472 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482520 1069 "q-125522" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482520 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482520 1071 "Find the $25$th percentile of the random variable $X$ if the probability distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}-1,\\, & 2<x<4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482520 1072 #uuid "2f4c7c35-e12b-53f3-8c46-ab140e4e9992" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482520 1074 "The $100p$th percentile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${x}_{p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {x}_{p})=p.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we want to find the $25$th percentile, which corresponds to the decimal $p=0.25.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {x}_{0.25}) & ={\\int }_{2}^{{x}_{0.25}}(\\frac{x}{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x)|}_{2}^{{x}_{0.25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{0.25}^{2}}{4}-{x}_{0.25}-(\\frac{(2)^{2}}{4}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{0.25}^{2}}{4}-{x}_{0.25}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $25$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}_{0.25}^{2}}{4}-{x}_{0.25}+1 & =0.25 \\\\ {x}_{0.25}^{2}-4{x}_{0.25}+3 & =0 \\\\ ({x}_{0.25}-3)({x}_{0.25}-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}_{0.25}=1,\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we take the root ${x}_{0.25}=3$ because $2<{x}_{0.25}<4.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482406 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482521 1069 "q-161755" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482521 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482521 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}xz({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482521 1072 #uuid "31ed4558-6b89-5259-81f7-c1113b514f8f" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482521 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=xz({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xz({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\rho }^{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482522 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482394 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482522 1069 "q-126226" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482522 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482522 1071 "![](images/08/08b3898f8aa867bfec0453da33cc8ae2d1f222d65029f45f09bf0beccb1dca62.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function $f(x)$ shown above. What is the mode of $X?$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482522 1072 #uuid "3249d5d6-dafe-59e8-a2d4-ffdd59e29afa" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482522 1074 "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that $f(x)$ has a single global maximum at $x=12.$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $12.$\n\n![](images/ed/ed9bc805045e01c7524fb32292b3728239432b9169bd1af148204804f9df700c.png)" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482523 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482430 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482523 1069 "q-111620" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482523 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482523 1071 "Factor ${p}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}p+\\frac{9}{16}.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482523 1072 #uuid "33ad6f1c-10b4-54e8-b066-192c2a7ae636" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482523 1074 "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}-2ap+{a}^{2}=(p-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}p+\\frac{9}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}p+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}p+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=\\frac{3}{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(p-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482524 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482451 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482524 1069 "q-97025" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482524 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482524 1071 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(2-x)(x+1)^{2}}{8}, & -2\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482524 1072 #uuid "3564d8be-ba15-5300-bf05-043e2a7c4f77" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482524 1074 "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-2\\le x\\le 2$ is $f(x)=\\frac{(2-x)(x+1)^{2}}{8}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{-(x+1)^{2}+2(2-x)(x+1)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(-x-1+4-2x)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(x+1)(1-x)}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3(x+1)(1-x)}{8}\\,⟹\\,x=-1,1.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[-2,2],$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=-2,2.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=-2,-1,1,2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =\\frac{(2+2)(-2+1)^{2}}{8}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(-1) & =\\frac{(2+1)(-1+1)^{2}}{8}=0 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{(2-1)(1+1)^{2}}{8}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{(2-2)(2+1)^{2}}{8}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-2)=f(1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-2$ and $1.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482396 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482525 1069 "q-125520" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482525 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482525 1071 "Find the $51.2$th percentile of the random variable $X$ if the probability distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482525 1072 #uuid "36945eec-4c74-5f74-8f42-dd9a1f9a922a" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482525 1074 "The $100p$th percentile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${x}_{p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {x}_{p})=p.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we want to find the $51.2$th percentile, which corresponds to the decimal $p=0.512.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {x}_{0.512}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{{x}_{0.512}}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{x}^{\\frac{3}{2}}|}_{0}^{{x}_{0.512}} \\\\  & ={x}_{0.512}^{\\frac{3}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $51.2$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0.512}^{\\frac{3}{2}} & =0.512 \\\\ {x}_{0.512}^{\\frac{1}{2}} & =\\sqrt[3]{0.512} \\\\ {x}_{0.512}^{\\frac{1}{2}} & =0.8 \\\\ {x}_{0.512} & =(0.8)^{2} \\\\ {x}_{0.512} & =0.64.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482526 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482437 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482526 1069 "q-214851" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482526 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482526 1071 "If $g(u,v)=3\\cdot {5}^{2u-3v}-2u,$ then $g(5,3)=${{field-1}}." 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482526 1072 #uuid "377f9da3-7e91-540b-b15e-84984d6004ce" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482526 1074 "We substitute $u=5$ and $v=3$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(u,v) & =3\\cdot {5}^{2u-3v}-2u \\\\ g(5,3) & =3\\cdot {5}^{2(5)-3(3)}-2(5) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {5}^{10-9}-10 \\\\  & =3\\cdot {5}^{1}-10 \\\\  & =15-10 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482527 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482424 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482527 1069 "q-2687" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482527 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482527 1071 "Factor the expression ${n}^{2}-8n+16.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482527 1072 #uuid "380d2267-0a37-5388-bacc-f5df7afa8e5c" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482527 1074 "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}-2an+{a}^{2}=(n-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}-8n+16.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-8n+{4}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-2\\cdot 4n+{4}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n-4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482528 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482456 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482528 1069 "q-69075" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482528 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482528 1071 "What is the value of $-0.5\\sqrt{20}$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482528 1072 #uuid "3a5045e4-621f-54ed-8866-51a959850017" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482528 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations: \n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, we first approximate the value of the radical using a calculator:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{20}≈‭‭4.472\\,136\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\nNow, we approximate the value of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-0.5\\sqrt{20} & ≈ \\\\ -0.5\\times ‭‭4.472\\,136 & ≈ \\\\ -‭2.236\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482529 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482428 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482529 1069 "q-111618" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482529 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482529 1071 "Factor the expression ${n}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}n+\\frac{25}{16}.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482529 1072 #uuid "3ab2e3ce-42ef-5550-a256-e8688e7ed48b" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482529 1074 "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}+2an+{a}^{2}=(n+a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}n+\\frac{25}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}n+{(\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}n+{(\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with $a=\\frac{5}{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(n+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482530 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482489 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482530 1069 "q-161894" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482530 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482530 1071 "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le x+y\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le y+z\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482530 1072 #uuid "3b7c4644-c6ca-51e8-b34c-e72d6ef6b667" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482530 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le u\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u]_{u=\\pi }^{u=2\\pi }\\cdot {[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]}_{v=0}^{v=\\pi }\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(-1+(-1))\\cdot (-(-1)+1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482466 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482531 1069 "q-109579" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482531 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482531 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482531 1072 #uuid "3c788761-8a3d-5a84-9fc6-a55521c1f3bb" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482531 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-3I:$\n\n$$\nA-3I=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)v=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)w=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482400 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482532 1069 "q-125734" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482532 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482532 1071 "Find the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<4, \\\\ 1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\ge 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482532 1072 #uuid "3ca6810e-a219-54cd-a8c8-f575cfccdc55" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482532 1074 "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{16}{{q}_{1}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{16}{{q}_{1}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {q}_{1} & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{16}{{q}_{3}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{16}{{q}_{3}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\nIQR={q}_{3}-{q}_{1}=8-\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482384 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482533 1069 "q-69121" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482533 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482533 1071 "$(-2)\\times (4.1-\\sqrt{4})=$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482533 1072 #uuid "442c94f9-fe8b-546a-8c5a-f7942d124959" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482533 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations: \n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)\\times (4.1-\\sqrt{4}) & = \\\\ (-2)\\times (4.1-\\sqrt{2\\cdot 2}) & = \\\\ (-2)\\times (4.1-\\sqrt{{2}^{2}}) & = \\\\ (-2)\\times (4.1-2) & = \\\\ (-2)\\times (2.1) & = \\\\ -4.2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482389 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482534 1069 "q-161871" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482534 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482534 1071 "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le xy\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=xy,\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\n \n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}y+3xyz)\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482534 1072 #uuid "46b1b539-1890-56ee-b628-8f298fa77da5" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482534 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=xy,\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ y & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(x-0) \\\\  & =3x \\\\  & =3u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}y+3xyz)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}(uv+vw)\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}(uv+vw)\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\frac{1}{3}(v+\\frac{vw}{u})\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482401 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482535 1069 "q-161789" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482535 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482535 1071 "![](images/08/085e28508da725e445c410fedc08a1992dccc5e0a7b99b3248182f58ffae2c07.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4z,$ shown above." 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482535 1072 #uuid "4913e4d1-197e-5ba9-a574-72530dddbf74" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482535 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,0,2).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4z$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4z \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/8a/8a730a72fe9f3f69878ef647215e8ed05e913f23bf8e0fab01bad6271e860335.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\rho }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\rho }\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ{[\\frac{1}{2}{\\rho }^{2}]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}\\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta  \\\\  & =8{[-\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{3}⁡ϕ]}_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi /2}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}(0-1)\\cdot (2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482536 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482483 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482536 1069 "q-161774" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482536 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482536 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=6$ for $x\\le 0$ and $z\\le 0.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482536 1072 #uuid "4a047214-1415-5946-9ec9-da29ccac571a" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482536 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $\\sqrt{6}$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/02/02834e98afa14d5193e5d560f85457e73caee7f5938af5ac8b3fb607165aad53.png)\n\n \nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{6},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482481 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482537 1069 "q-69265" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482537 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482537 1071 "What is the value of $2\\sqrt{(3-6)\\times (1-3)}$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482537 1072 #uuid "4ae171d6-ef6c-513e-bb69-e2d14e0a590f" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482537 1074 "To simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations: \n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{(3-6)\\times (1-3)} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{(-3)\\times (-2)} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we approximate the value of the radical using a calculator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & ≈‭‭‭2.449\\,490\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\nFinally, we approximate the value of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{6} & ≈ \\\\ 2(2.449\\,490) & ≈ \\\\ 4.898\\,98 & ≈ \\\\ 4.899\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482502 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482538 1069 "q-111370" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482538 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482538 1071 "${(3w+2v)}^{2}=$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482538 1072 #uuid "4b635fc4-723b-5734-9d78-9acb6ad51368" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482538 1074 "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{2}={a}^{2}+2ab+{b}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=3w$ and $b=2v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3w+2v)}^{2} & = \\\\ {(3w)}^{2}+2(3w)(2v)+{(2v)}^{2} & = \\\\ 9{w}^{2}+12vw+4{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482448 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482539 1069 "q-334797" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482539 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482539 1071 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $3.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.25$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.25)≈0.125.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.10,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.10)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482539 1072 #uuid "4b679574-cdae-5555-900f-72dfd634fac3" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482539 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.25$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.25}{0.10}=2.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3}=(2.5)^{3}=15.625.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.10,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈\\frac{0.125}{15.625}=0.008.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈0.008.\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482457 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482540 1069 "q-161889" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482540 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482540 1071 "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,2\\le x-y\\le 3,\\,1\\le {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,1\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}6z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482540 1072 #uuid "4f86e12d-91a6-559a-805d-f5f0798ca229" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482540 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 4,\\,3\\le w\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2x & -2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2x & -2y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(-2y+2x) \\\\  & =6(x-y) \\\\  & =6u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}6z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}2w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{6u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{3u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{3u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482414 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482541 1069 "q-171401" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482541 1071 "Rounded to $3$ decimal places, $\\sqrt{6+4\\times 2}=${{field-1}}." 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482541 1072 #uuid "531dca87-ee51-5a1e-9c95-aee21eccb0e1" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482541 1074 "To simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations: \n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6+4\\times 2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{6+8} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we approximate the value of $\\sqrt{14}$ using a calculator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & ≈‭‭‭3.742\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482484 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482542 1069 "q-126179" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482542 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482542 1071 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 3 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482542 1072 #uuid "5361d7e0-4877-5c07-8e5e-b4d8b7ac0426" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482542 1074 "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 3$ is $f(x)=\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{9}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{9}x=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nThe point $x=0$ is in the interval $[0,3],$ so we keep it.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,3.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {0}^{2}=0 \\\\ f(3) & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {3}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(3)=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $3.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482543 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482543 1069 "q-94670" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482543 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482543 1071 "Find the $21$st percentile of the random variable $X$ if the probability distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{21}x,\\, & 2\\le x\\le 5, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482543 1072 #uuid "537b608f-d718-5a66-8b0b-7dabfac0375b" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482543 1074 "The $100p$th percentile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${x}_{p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {x}_{p})=p.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we want to find the $21$st percentile, which corresponds to the decimal $p=0.21.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {x}_{0.21}) & ={\\int }_{2}^{{x}_{0.21}}\\frac{2}{21}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{21}|}_{2}^{{x}_{0.21}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{0.21}^{2}}{21}-\\frac{4}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $21$st percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}_{0.21}^{2}}{21}-\\frac{4}{21} & =0.21 \\\\ {x}_{0.21}^{2} & =(0.21)(21)+4 \\\\ {x}_{0.21} & =2.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482544 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482385 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482544 1069 "q-132221" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482544 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482544 1071 "![](images/a6/a68077b18f8c08fc7cd68889b6472373c7677bcfcb6f370abce1e93cf67c4594.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the line $x=0,$ and the curves $y=5-{x}^{2}$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dA=$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482544 1072 #uuid "61556a8f-0a92-5ecd-8c20-5948347f1756" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482544 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}x & =5-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-x-10 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(2x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard the solution $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ since $x\\le 0$ in our region. Therefore, $x$-limits are $-2\\le x\\le 0.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/5b/5bafe1d8cd35eec83aba3a9a24f777340a09f824919d4cedc5879c774e30cf5e.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}x\\le y\\le 5-{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-x/2}^{5-{x}^{2}}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482399 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482450 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482473 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482545 1069 "q-216500" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482545 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482545 1071 "${(7{m}^{2}+4n)}^{2}=${{field-1}}${m}^{4}\\,\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${m}^{2}n\\,\\,+$\n{{field-3}}${n}^{2}$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482545 1072 #uuid "6624a980-6efe-5c9a-82eb-5aebfbc29594" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482545 1074 "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{2}={a}^{2}+2ab+{b}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=7{m}^{2}$ and $b=4n,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(7{m}^{2}+4n)}^{2} & = \\\\ {(7{m}^{2})}^{2}+2(7{m}^{2})(4n)+(4n)^{2} & = \\\\ 49{m}^{4}+56{m}^{2}n+16{n}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482546 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482499 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482546 1069 "q-126224" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482546 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482546 1071 "![](images/cd/cdd4ace952374430c34d74693d68b06875b6c8f998542ddd30342df178a31057.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function $f(x)$ shown above. What is the mode of $X?$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482546 1072 #uuid "68f249e7-d40f-5592-ac60-248ad19377c7" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482546 1074 "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that $f(x)$ has a single global maximum at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $2.$\n\n![](images/72/72046f8f852b5fc53226fa611718011f2f8b58ee0672bfd8862ad506548156cb.png)" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482547 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482500 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482547 1069 "q-162183" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482547 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482547 1071 "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,-2\\le x-z\\le 0,\\,1\\le \\frac{z-1}{y}\\le 5\\}.\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482547 1072 #uuid "7144ca6a-debb-5e59-baf7-8232b8798b6c" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482547 1074 "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y\\le 3,\\,-2\\le x-z\\le 0,\\,1\\le \\frac{z-1}{y}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y,\\,v=x-z,\\,w=\\frac{z-1}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-2\\le v\\le 0,\\,1\\le w\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{z-1}{{y}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=u.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |u|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{5}u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{-2}^{0}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{5}dw \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{u=1}^{u=3}\\cdot [v]_{v=-2}^{v=0}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (0-(-2))\\cdot (5-1) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482548 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482390 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482548 1069 "q-94628" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482548 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482548 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482548 1072 #uuid "714df0ea-5d53-53df-b0aa-14ba037b9bde" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482548 1074 "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le m) & ={\\int }_{1}^{m}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{m}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{-1/2}{|}_{1}^{m} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{m}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{m}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{m}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{m} & =2 \\\\ m & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482549 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482465 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482549 1069 "q-140949" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482549 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482549 1071 "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482549 1072 #uuid "72a8e42a-c396-5007-82f3-4a7623931a98" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482549 1074 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482386 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482550 1069 "q-95328" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482550 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482550 1071 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482550 1072 #uuid "72c83079-fa51-5573-830e-81ba6d3220b1" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482550 1074 "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 2$ is $f(x)=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions  of  ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,2.$ So, these are critical points.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{0}{2}=0 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{2}{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(2)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $2.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482551 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482467 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482551 1069 "q-125515" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482551 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482551 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<2, \\\\ \\frac{1}{240}({x}^{4}-16), & 2\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482551 1072 #uuid "72db930f-dc67-5772-9a58-607de1ef27a6" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482551 1074 "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=F(m)=\\frac{1}{240}({m}^{4}-16),\\,2\\le m\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{240}({m}^{4}-16)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,m=\\sqrt[4]{136}.\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $2\\le m\\le 4.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482552 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482412 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482552 1069 "q-161921" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482552 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482552 1071 "![](images/54/54ff64195ccfadddfe2b3ca0bb9e193147fedae20d8dae6a53a5ecddc3c26f5d.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=-16y$ for $x\\le 0$ and $z\\ge 0,$ shown above. \nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482552 1072 #uuid "75e6fbad-2ffa-554a-b73e-206d1de35163" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482552 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$  represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $8$ centered at $(0,-8,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=-16y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =-16y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =-16\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/86/868214a1c2b30da5b16b7f0c307b369bdc9f2d2201ee0ad5a3f5da2a8bdc6f49.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ} \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482553 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482474 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482553 1069 "q-95193" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482553 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482553 1071 "![](images/a3/a3e7de4f59d37e0fab767b979d9652844fa8960a1c6e9f0ba572cc4de916bc2a.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown above. What are the modes of $X?$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482553 1072 #uuid "7a19bcdd-15af-5ef0-a245-ee1a2c98862d" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482553 1074 "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that the global maxima of $f(x)$ are at $x=5,10.$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $5$ and $10.$\n\n![](images/d7/d70343741f05f43de9db3624eda40e9ed003819db2cb54e37a3392a2887362da.png)" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482421 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482422 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482426 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482554 1069 "q-216524" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482554 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482554 1071 "${(4m-3{n}^{2})}^{2}=${{field-1}}${m}^{2}\\,\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$m{n}^{2}\\,\\,+$\n{{field-3}}${n}^{4}$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482554 1072 #uuid "7c0de6db-459c-5036-9e1c-862780d7a093" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482554 1074 "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a-b)^{2}={a}^{2}-2ab+{b}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=4m$ and $b=3{n}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(4m-3{n}^{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ {(4m)}^{2}-2(4m)(3{n}^{2})+{(3{n}^{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ 16{m}^{2}-24m{n}^{2}+9{n}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482555 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482382 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482555 1069 "q-95195" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482555 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482555 1071 "![](images/9d/9de32e1e1f07e531f6c86419640da24288bc137cad29860e789774df812aa283.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown above. What are the modes of $X?$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482555 1072 #uuid "7c410d24-cb7b-59e5-b706-34c806e9c72e" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482555 1074 "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that the global maxima of $f(x)$ are at $x=3,9.$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $3$  and $9.$\n\n![](images/4c/4cc925bf2013c4af687cd917bd8550a4bce7b9f1d7ae0de404af36db567333a7.png)" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482556 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482495 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482556 1069 "q-125712" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482556 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482556 1071 "Find, to three decimal places, the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0, \\\\ {x}^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 1,\\, & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482556 1072 #uuid "7e41b2e9-6ace-571f-a3ef-6f43b41df0ec" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482556 1074 "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {q}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {q}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we take the root ${q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ because $0\\le {q}_{1}\\le 1.$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {q}_{3}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we take the root ${q}_{3}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ because $0\\le {q}_{3}\\le 1.$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}IQR & ={q}_{3}-{q}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2} \\\\  & ≈0.366\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482557 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482446 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482557 1069 "q-69087" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482557 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482557 1071 "What is the value of $(-2)\\sqrt{16}+3.3?$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482557 1072 #uuid "7f36415e-84fb-5eb9-a3cb-42107c1ff469" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482557 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations: \n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, we first evaluate the value of the radical, and we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)\\sqrt{16}+3.3 & = \\\\ (-2)\\sqrt{4\\cdot 4}+3.3 & = \\\\ (-2)\\sqrt{{4}^{2}}+3.3 & = \\\\ (-2)\\cdot 4+3.3 & = \\\\ -8+3.3 & = \\\\ -4.7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482482 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482558 1069 "q-161762" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482558 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=36.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482558 1072 #uuid "80477a9d-e7af-5d9c-aba6-cf99ec36f94b" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482558 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $6$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =((\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2})\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482493 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482559 1069 "q-69112" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482559 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482559 1071 "What is $\\sqrt{{3}^{3}÷({2}^{3}+1)\\times 27}?$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482559 1072 #uuid "833d1e11-5a78-5410-a7b5-c29285538e0f" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482559 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations: \n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{3}^{3}÷({2}^{3}+1)\\times 27} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{3}^{3}÷(8+1)\\times 27} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{3}^{3}÷9\\times 27} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{27÷9\\times 27} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{3\\times 27} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{81} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9\\cdot 9} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{9}^{2}} & = \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482388 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482560 1069 "q-101234" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482560 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482560 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the expression $7p-t+2r?$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482560 1072 #uuid "8350b8fc-cebc-5d70-a81a-fd2cc02c4b2c" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482560 1074 "The expression $7p-t+2r$ has three terms:  $7p$, $-t$, and $2r.$ \n\nThe order in which we combine the terms doesn't matter. Therefore, we can also write this expression as \n\n$$\n7p+2r-t.\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482561 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482453 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482561 1069 "q-35796" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482561 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482561 1071 "Find the value of $f(t)=(t+1)^{2}-2(t+1)$ at $t=-2.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482561 1072 #uuid "86c185f8-7069-5803-a8a3-6aebf9cf8dbc" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482561 1074 "We substitute $t=-2$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =(t+1)^{2}-2(t+1) \\\\ f(-2) & =(-2+1)^{2}-2(-2+1) \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}-2(-1) \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482562 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482387 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482562 1069 "q-125773" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482562 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482562 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{98}{{x}^{3}},\\, & x\\ge 7, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482562 1072 #uuid "8d19c0b1-13a1-5100-8957-4c982f273723" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482562 1074 "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le m) & ={\\int }_{7}^{m}\\frac{98}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{49}{{x}^{2}}|}_{7}^{m} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{49}{{m}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{49}{{m}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{49}{{m}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ m & =7\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $m>7.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482563 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482461 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482563 1069 "q-43627" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482563 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482563 1071 "Find ${3}^{2}÷(\\sqrt{25}-2).$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482563 1072 #uuid "8d68d1bd-9a61-5ae6-b9dd-a03d4f1484bc" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482563 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations: \n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2}÷(\\sqrt{25}-2) & = \\\\ {3}^{2}÷(\\sqrt{5\\cdot 5}-2) & = \\\\ {3}^{2}÷(\\sqrt{{5}^{2}}-2) & = \\\\ {3}^{2}÷(5-2) & = \\\\ {3}^{2}÷3 & = \\\\ 9÷3 & = \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482564 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482449 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482564 1069 "q-126225" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482564 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482564 1071 "![](images/c6/c6dbd0ce5bee5ce6b40308f8b75d4dd1c85c3d704faa152f4efe15443aae6894.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function $f(x)$ shown above. What is the mode of $X?$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482564 1072 #uuid "90278635-fce6-5765-ae39-6533399724f4" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482564 1074 "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that $f(x)$ has a single global maximum at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $0.$\n\n![](images/ec/ec6675a5ad54923d1e0ce1b2159759d8b8be70cb4b20e82c1694c58ab49d0fb5.png)" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482565 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482460 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482565 1069 "q-126323" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482565 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482565 1071 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3x},\\, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482565 1072 #uuid "90f3bc1d-94f7-5716-a7c9-9b34d22c275c" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482565 1074 "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $x\\ge 0$ is $f(x)=3{e}^{-3x}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-9{e}^{-3x}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions  of  ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoint $x=0.$ So, this is the only critical point.\n\nSince ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for $x>0,$ then $f(0)$ is the global maximum of $f.$ Therefore, the mode of $X$ is $0.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482566 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482455 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482566 1069 "q-161918" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482566 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482566 1071 "![](images/11/110e46158875a0aeb3b3aa898b886ee03b8c4cf945e9853a4962e839c5b1f0f6.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=-10y,$ shown above. \nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482566 1072 #uuid "934f832d-975f-5cb5-827f-5f4ce2444543" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482566 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $5$ centered at $(0,-5,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=-10y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =-10y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =-10\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/8f/8f2c3d4810b8364e17ad82a0d271d9affe2714938bd7cccbc69c6174e1f03ec4.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}{{\\rho }^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482567 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482410 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482567 1069 "q-111614" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482567 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482567 1071 "Factor $-24f+144+{f}^{2}.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482567 1072 #uuid "94bbfbab-f0f9-56d8-b645-4168ecf299d7" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482567 1074 "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{f}^{2}-2af+{a}^{2}=(f-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n-24f+144+{f}^{2}={f}^{2}-24f+144\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{2}-24f+{12}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{2}-2\\cdot 12f+{12}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=12.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f-12)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482503 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482568 1069 "q-95161" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482568 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482568 1071 "![](images/db/db23d650308c0b217548eac5e3976cee561d90845eaa5be6fc07174e64d8ebf0.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function $f(x)$ shown above. What is the mode of $X?$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482568 1072 #uuid "95801bee-5916-59b8-993a-e9a5631d930a" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482568 1074 "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that $f(x)$ has a single global maximum at $x=7.$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $7.$\n\n![](images/a8/a814c66be550f9850a9807a19d3f0dc836b3fba08c2cd3b451dc00f94b3e4d24.png)" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482569 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482492 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482569 1069 "q-130589" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482569 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482569 1071 "Evaluate $f(-3,0)$ if $f(x,y)={5}^{x+4}+x\\cdot {2}^{y+1}.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482569 1072 #uuid "9ddffbc5-a8dd-53fa-a3bc-4cc663a782e3" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482569 1074 "We substitute $x=-3$ and $y=0$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={5}^{x+4}+x\\cdot {2}^{y+1} \\\\ f(-3,0) & ={5}^{-3+4}+(-3)\\cdot {2}^{0+1} \\\\  & ={5}^{1}+(-3)\\cdot {2}^{1} \\\\  & ={5}^{1}-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5-6 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482380 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482570 1069 "q-94601" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482570 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482570 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{144}, & 0\\le x\\le 12, \\\\ 1, & x>12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482570 1072 #uuid "a12ce635-7077-5ae6-9899-fd940aea64f8" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482570 1074 "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=F(m)=\\frac{{m}^{2}}{144},\\,0\\le m\\le 12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{m}^{2}}{144}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,m=6\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $0\\le m\\le 12.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482477 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482571 1069 "q-125714" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482571 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482571 1071 "Find, to three decimal places, the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{8}, & 1\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 1, & x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482571 1072 #uuid "a4e71952-c18f-5e5a-8bbb-dfd0f7ad678f" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482571 1074 "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{q}_{1}^{2}-1}{8} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {q}_{1}^{2}-1 & =2 \\\\ {q}_{1}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {q}_{1} & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we take the root ${q}_{1}=\\sqrt{3}$ because $1\\le {q}_{1}\\le 3.$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{q}_{3}^{2}-1}{8} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {q}_{3}^{2}-1 & =6 \\\\ {q}_{3}^{2} & =7 \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we take the root ${q}_{3}=\\sqrt{7}$ because $1\\le {q}_{3}\\le 3.$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}IQR & ={q}_{3}-{q}_{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & ≈0.914\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482572 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482459 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482572 1069 "q-95190" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482572 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482572 1071 "![](images/04/0488c64b17d96c9b5c25fee5d321345cbb346b5f7b448c1243fed71a9c00f809.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown above. What are the modes of $X?$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482572 1072 #uuid "a54b50d2-00a6-51c8-a8ca-b8464cbdd7da" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482572 1074 "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that the global maxima of $f(x)$ are at $x=4,10.$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $4$ and $10.$\n\n![](images/69/6991ad6712c354ba4821a6a7ec717560cfa1cfab9b45e029b762307b706e48b1.png)" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482411 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482573 1069 "q-95180" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482573 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482573 1071 "![](images/aa/aa2638a96ae91d0b2a8a0d1216e8256c7823c231c5ceecd9a4b3fd14c239ed53.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function $f(x)$ shown above. What is the mode of $X?$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482573 1072 #uuid "a5a77e1a-841f-57c5-8990-3f339cc0802e" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482573 1074 "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that $f(x)$ has a single global maximum at $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $4.$\n\n![](images/52/52c2514b1970553a6b4f80138e4baa6b8384d482caa447b95bd07dde5c252d11.png)" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482574 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482462 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482574 1069 "q-31666" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482574 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482574 1071 "Evaluate $g(2),$ if $g(x)=(x-3)(x+4)(x-7).$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482574 1072 #uuid "a662c339-4277-548c-aaa6-345c7b02ceaa" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482574 1074 "We substitute $x=2$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =(x-3)(x+4)(x-7) \\\\ g(2) & =(2-3)(2+4)(2-7) \\\\  & =(-1)(6)(-5) \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482575 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482476 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482575 1069 "q-94643" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482575 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1, \\\\ \\frac{6}{{x}^{7}},\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482575 1072 #uuid "a6d47759-e842-511b-a810-c9880823e33f" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482575 1074 "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le m) & ={\\int }_{1}^{m}\\frac{6}{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}|}_{1}^{m} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{m}^{6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{m}^{6}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{m}^{6}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ m & =\\sqrt[6]{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $m\\ge 1.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482464 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482576 1069 "q-161750" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482576 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482576 1072 #uuid "ac302ee6-6bbe-5388-bf60-4247113c7536" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482576 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482425 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482577 1069 "q-69750" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482577 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482577 1071 "What is $\\sqrt{{(-36)}^{2}}?$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482577 1072 #uuid "ad6df804-3e3c-5d45-acbc-edbd4509746d" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482577 1074 "Remember that by definition, the square root is always zero or positive, never negative.\n\nSo, the square root comes out to\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{(-36)^{2}}=\\sqrt{{36}^{2}}=36.\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482479 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482578 1069 "q-33092" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482578 1071 "${(\\frac{p}{2}+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}=$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482578 1072 #uuid "ae2cfcdb-b465-5141-b188-02dc5998e9f5" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482578 1074 "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{2}={a}^{2}+2ab+{b}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=\\frac{p}{2}$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{p}{2}+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ {(\\frac{p}{2})}^{2}+2(\\frac{p}{2})(\\frac{3}{2})+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{{p}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{3}{2}p+\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482440 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482579 1069 "q-44087" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482579 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482579 1071 "$16x+64+{x}^{2}=$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482579 1072 #uuid "b2c7f8cb-2d06-5a06-8c22-c48f3e03b4b4" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482579 1074 "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2ax+{a}^{2}=(x+a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n16x+64+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}+16x+64.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+16x+{8}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 8x+{8}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with $a=8.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+8)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482432 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482580 1069 "q-162211" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482580 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482580 1071 "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\le 2\\}.\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482580 1072 #uuid "b4b2886a-abaf-53c5-8dbb-583291c4a37e" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482580 1074 "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=y,\\,w=z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1} & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=2} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482436 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482581 1069 "q-99397" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482581 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482581 1071 "$\\sqrt{4÷({3}^{2}-5)}=$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482581 1072 #uuid "b558035c-db2a-5cb8-b598-445d3a220fdc" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482581 1074 "To simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations: \n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4÷({3}^{2}-5)} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{4÷(9-5)} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{4÷4} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{1} & = \\\\ 1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482391 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482392 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482582 1069 "q-335436" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482582 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482582 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(3) \\\\ y(3)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"140px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482582 1072 #uuid "b5cac624-fcaa-5546-985b-94ad2df95205" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482582 1074 "% Explanation\nWriting our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-3)}\\,x(3) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6(t-3)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6(t-3)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6(t-3)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6(t-3))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6(t-3))\\, \\\\ \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6(t-3))\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482438 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482583 1069 "q-161743" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482583 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482583 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482583 1072 #uuid "b6dfd2f6-e989-5dc0-8a5f-19807faa0173" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482583 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482584 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482470 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482584 1069 "q-80541" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482584 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482584 1071 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y=2{x}^{2}+1$ and $y=3.$ If $R=[-1,1]\\times [1,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482584 1072 #uuid "b79622ff-7b5d-5073-9074-7493234a4c4c" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482584 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/1c/1ca0b430b798a6dc85fd2b731a93690f79bb2c45b33bbe6f31a5618a2ce0888a.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and II equal $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function III. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,1)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482418 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482585 1069 "q-161922" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482585 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482585 1071 "![](images/cf/cf44412afd6f2a8eaab468676b4f6a9828f5000eec287234f9834a41e6816d5a.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=20x$ for $z\\le 0,$ shown above.\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482585 1072 #uuid "ba16213a-d9f2-5675-b515-f66161bd7267" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482585 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$  represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $10$ centered at $(10,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=20x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =20x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =20\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -20\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =20\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/16/165ed50d8f94f3b896f4d19647fb514f544221872a6df241a6ab6c3797bbd698.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 20\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{{(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{20\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482441 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482586 1069 "q-85285" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482586 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482586 1071 "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are shown below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482586 1072 #uuid "bbdebe64-e032-577f-8298-161c9dc3fe78" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482586 1074 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}}=\\sqrt{35}.\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482398 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482587 1069 "q-161768" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482587 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482587 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ for $x\\ge 0$ and $z\\le 0.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482587 1072 #uuid "bbdf7ac8-9c8d-5122-94b8-607404da611a" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482587 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/54/540ae5200e5628d721a64b2ee5ad2b3f0e3f7c3192ccb1f90aeab71abc93f070.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{({\\rho }^{2})}^{3}} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{3}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482395 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482588 1069 "q-126721" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482588 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482588 1071 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482588 1072 #uuid "c069136c-9e24-5901-bcc3-c8fd76fb24b5" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482588 1074 "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $x\\ge 0$ is $f(x)=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{(1+x)^{3}}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions of  ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoint $x=0.$ So, this is the only critical point.\n\nSince ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for $x>0,$ then $f(0)$ is the global maximum of $f(x).$\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $0.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482413 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482589 1069 "q-136201" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482589 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482589 1071 "$7\\sqrt{6÷(6-{2}^{2})}=$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482589 1072 #uuid "c552807e-b3ea-557b-87d6-077887001adc" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482589 1074 "To simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations: \n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\sqrt{6÷(6-{2}^{2})} & = \\\\ 7\\sqrt{6÷(6-4)} & = \\\\ 7\\sqrt{6÷2} & = \\\\ 7\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482442 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482590 1069 "q-161773" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482590 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482590 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2$ for $x\\le 0$ and $z\\ge 0.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482590 1072 #uuid "ca85c537-9f01-5979-bc36-583972546691" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482590 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/c4/c4d31af830ee7d166662e88b1a7ade0c817d2508b887c15db5838d25b9467fca.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & ={({\\rho }^{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{4}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\rho }^{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482591 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482415 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482591 1069 "q-94598" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482591 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482591 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}, & 0\\le x\\le 5, \\\\ 1, & x>5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482591 1072 #uuid "cc24bfb9-272f-54fa-b546-5cdc700f6030" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482591 1074 "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=F(m)=\\frac{{m}^{2}}{25},\\,0\\le m\\le 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{m}^{2}}{25}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $0\\le m\\le 5.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482383 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482592 1069 "q-95380" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482592 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482592 1071 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{2}, & -1\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482592 1072 #uuid "cc6f0c6d-fbfc-515f-9faf-cdb2e36bd29a" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482592 1074 "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-1\\le x\\le 1$ is $f(x)=\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =10{x}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n10{x}^{3}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nThis critical point is within the interval $[-1,1],$ so we keep it.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=-1,1.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=-1,0,1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =\\frac{5\\cdot (-1)^{4}}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ f(0) & =\\frac{5\\cdot {0}^{4}}{2}=0 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{5\\cdot {1}^{4}}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-1)=f(1)=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-1$ and $1.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482593 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482420 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482593 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482431 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482593 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482454 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482593 1069 "q-216505" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482593 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482593 1071 "${(3{m}^{4}+5n)}^{2}=${{field-1}}${m}^{8}\\,\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${m}^{4}n\\,\\,+$\n{{field-3}}${n}^{2}$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482593 1072 #uuid "cd828af1-4191-51f3-be77-cb7614ed0a33" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482593 1074 "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{2}={a}^{2}+2ab+{b}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=3{m}^{4}$ and $b=5n,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3{m}^{4}+5n)}^{2} & = \\\\ {(3{m}^{4})}^{2}+2(3{m}^{4})(5n)+{(5n)}^{2} & = \\\\ 9{m}^{8}+30{m}^{4}n+25{n}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482452 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482594 1069 "q-31200" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482594 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482594 1071 "${({y}^{3}-2)}^{2}=$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482594 1072 #uuid "cff0df09-cba2-54ca-961e-ef5673e7180f" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482594 1074 "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a-b)^{2}={a}^{2}-2ab+{b}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a={y}^{3}$ and $b=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{3}-2)^{2} & = \\\\ {({y}^{3})}^{2}-2({y}^{3})(2)+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{6}-4{y}^{3}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482595 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482402 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482595 1069 "q-95191" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482595 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482595 1071 "![](images/7c/7c41fd3e5730b563acdd30f13853b1dfa5111cb16fb8b7f32fa746f58b515538.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown above. What are the modes of $X?$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482595 1072 #uuid "d092f972-98cf-5c56-8546-b76547cbb36a" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482595 1074 "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that the global maxima of $f(x)$ are at $x=1,5,10.$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $1,5$ and $10.$\n\n![](images/d8/d82edc89a519c6286f665c217871965b889697e87c3a4d6176f2e29e8b7596ac.png)" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482596 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482596 1069 "q-95348" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482596 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482596 1071 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{100}(5+4x-{x}^{2}), & 0\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482596 1072 #uuid "d0fd2e28-4af0-53c5-9c68-348af97ab34c" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482596 1074 "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 5$ is $f(x)=\\frac{3}{100}(5+4x-{x}^{2}).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{3}{100}(4-2x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{100}(4-2x)=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nThe point $x=2$ is in the interval $[0,5],$ so we keep it.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,5.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,2,5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{3}{100}(5+4\\cdot 0-{0}^{2})=\\frac{15}{100}=\\frac{3}{20} \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{3}{100}(5+4\\cdot 2-{2}^{2})=\\frac{27}{100} \\\\ f(5) & =\\frac{3}{100}(5+4\\cdot 5-{5}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(2)=\\frac{27}{100}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $2.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482597 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482435 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482597 1069 "q-86895" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482597 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482597 1071 "![](images/f5/f5f6dae762414eb327b9eae9e5445ef1916e9efc0e17c1316339bbeb416ec94e.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}y-x, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ y-x, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}y-x, & (x,y)\\in R∖D, \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482597 1072 #uuid "d267d682-18e1-5263-9cb3-12f52098c9f0" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482597 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and the rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/97/976dc1b0a7298c5cf0a331acb00594c8e4c74a14b6df3fc18f7e7997db2956c4.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $y-x$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions I and III. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,0)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function II equals $y-x$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482598 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482480 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482598 1069 "q-335266" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482598 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482598 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=9y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=9x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482598 1072 #uuid "d66735c3-ba98-5f1c-8e1d-7d80133c59cf" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482598 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482599 1069 "q-161933" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482599 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482599 1071 "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y+z,\\,v=x+4z,\\,w=2y\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\,\\frac{y+z}{x+4z}\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y+z\\le 2,\\,1\\le x+4z\\le e,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482599 1072 #uuid "d8c9e8ae-5eea-50e8-acfe-85f90d5a9922" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482599 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y+z,\\,v=x+4z,\\,w=2y\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le e,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-1)(-1). \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{y+z}{x+4z}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{u}{2v}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{u}{2}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{4}]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot {[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]}_{v=1}^{v=e}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=2} \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482600 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482433 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482600 1069 "q-71565" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482600 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482600 1071 "What is $\\sqrt{{(-121)}^{2}}?$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482600 1072 #uuid "da6d460c-dde7-5fc1-ac50-c5ee168f1351" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482600 1074 "Remember that by definition, the square root is always zero or positive, never negative.\n\nSo, the square root comes out to\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{(-121)^{2}}=\\sqrt{{121}^{2}}=121.\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482601 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482439 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482601 1069 "q-161777" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482601 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482601 1071 "![](images/90/90d5e9115d216f2a7f8fba49295a36b95cd932c4df7f8bace1b45e3542e9ae11.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{3}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ for $x\\ge 0$ and $z\\ge 0,$ shown above." 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482601 1072 #uuid "ddc59e97-e243-5e27-9d57-5e0f9b714882" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482601 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/ec/ec2f6c67eee7f836cc71efec2b17c2276aa8a9d397c632b3c499a46a75f7b95c.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 1,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & ={({\\rho }^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{3}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{6}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{8}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi /2}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =-\\pi /2}^{\\theta =\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{9}{\\rho }^{9}]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =1} \\\\  & =-(0-1)\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}(1-0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482602 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482397 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482602 1069 "q-94665" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482602 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482602 1071 "Find, to three decimal places, the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1, \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482602 1072 #uuid "dfe1ae13-0566-5996-84a0-b50d07468365" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482602 1074 "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{{q}_{1}}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{{q}_{1}}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {q}_{1} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{{q}_{3}}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{{q}_{3}}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\nIQR={q}_{3}-{q}_{1}=2-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}≈0.845\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482603 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482404 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482603 1069 "q-136194" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482603 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482603 1071 "$2\\sqrt{({5}^{2}+3)÷2}=$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482603 1072 #uuid "e0ce2975-3d0e-5df0-82ca-80c1fcc3d613" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482603 1074 "To simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations: \n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{({5}^{2}+3)÷2} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{(25+3)÷2} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{28÷2} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482468 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482604 1069 "q-78931" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482604 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482604 1071 "${({x}^{4}+2{c}^{2})}^{2}=$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482604 1072 #uuid "e5bdd830-1ec1-554e-a24b-2d538b13172e" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482604 1074 "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{2}={a}^{2}+2ab+{b}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a={x}^{4}$ and $b=2{c}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{4}+2{c}^{2})^{2} & = \\\\ {({x}^{4})}^{2}+2({x}^{4})(2{c}^{2})+(2{c}^{2})^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{8}+4{x}^{4}{c}^{2}+4{c}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482605 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482417 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482605 1069 "q-40890" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482605 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482605 1071 "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482605 1072 #uuid "e65b2b16-1d73-53d7-b99e-0f6611b54099" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482605 1074 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482606 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482407 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482606 1069 "q-162140" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482606 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482606 1071 "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+{y}^{2},\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(x+{y}^{2})^{9}z\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,0\\le y+z\\le 5,\\,0\\le z\\le 4\\}.\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482606 1072 #uuid "e662cc33-8ce5-5a82-9731-097b3595e6c3" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482606 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+{y}^{2},\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 5,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+{y}^{2})^{9}z\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{9}w\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{9}w\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{u}^{9}w\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{9}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{5}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}w\\,dw \\\\  & =[\\frac{{u}^{10}}{10}]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot {[v]}_{v=0}^{v=5}\\cdot [\\frac{{w}^{2}}{2}]_{w=0}^{w=4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{10}-0)\\cdot (5-0)\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482419 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482607 1069 "q-161861" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482607 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482607 1071 "![](images/75/759ebc78c794f433bba8c1f555bc68b2da52de62741711f13bd9c9156e3c485e.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=-2x,$ shown above. \nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482607 1072 #uuid "e9ac6b1f-2b21-59f9-9ae8-01d7811a1b26" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482607 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $1$ centered at $(-1,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=-2x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =-2x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =-2\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/43/43e3c386086d30431405bb3fe2ca2835595641041dd7e098f9234cab8e7b225d.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\rho }^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{{\\rho }^{2}}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482608 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482443 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482608 1069 "q-130203" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482608 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482608 1071 "Consider the plane curve ${y}^{2}=1-{x}^{2}+2y.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,2).$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482608 1072 #uuid "eaaccc28-17cb-56b1-b96e-6187ebe91480" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482608 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =1-{x}^{2}+2y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,2)={f}_{y}(-1,2)i-{f}_{x}(-1,2)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y)j \\\\  & =(2y-2)i-2x\\,j \\\\ t(-1,2) & =(2(2)-2)i-2(-1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $2\\,i+2\\,j.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482469 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482609 1069 "q-101231" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482609 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482609 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the expression $5b-5+t?$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482609 1072 #uuid "eb6d88d8-f577-5f53-98e7-edda212d8c82" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482609 1074 "The expression $5b-5+t$ has three terms:  $5b$, $-5$, and $t.$ \n\nThe order in which we combine the terms doesn't matter. Therefore, we can also write this expression as \n\n$$\nt-5+5b.\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482610 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482445 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482610 1069 "q-126294" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482610 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482610 1071 "![](images/f6/f6f2ce8adb8c1b35c2fd4928805f486920228b913c3a7fc18ddc60b26a8e8fcd.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown above. What are the modes of $X?$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482610 1072 #uuid "ed1f11b0-312f-5ca9-81fd-de20b37867fa" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482610 1074 "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that the global maxima of $f(x)$ are at $x=5,7.$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $5$ and $7.$\n\n![](images/59/59b72efa72dd48bc2fb74fed5f795341f59af377af0756965abab9d23390d468.png)" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482611 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482488 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482611 1069 "q-140950" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482611 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482611 1071 "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482611 1072 #uuid "f16e5a65-b3a9-5e9e-afd2-4d31b6ccc499" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482611 1074 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{198}[\\begin{aligned}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482612 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482444 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482612 1069 "q-31845" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482612 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482612 1071 "${x}^{2}+100-20x$ is equivalent to" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482612 1072 #uuid "f18cf588-d703-5104-bc39-415b94a76959" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482612 1074 "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+100-20x={x}^{2}-20x+100.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-20x+{10}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 10x+{10}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=10.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-10)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482494 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482613 1069 "q-94639" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482613 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482613 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\frac{3}{8{x}^{4}},\\, & x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482613 1072 #uuid "f4cab15e-7525-590b-aa74-c49be38d3cec" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482613 1074 "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le m) & ={\\int }_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{m}\\frac{3}{8{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8{x}^{3}}{|}_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{m} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{8{m}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{8{m}^{3}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8{m}^{3}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {m}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ m & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482409 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482463 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482475 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482614 1069 "q-337119" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482614 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482614 1071 "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,c$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $x({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by the fact that {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L6}$ by\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by {{field-3}}." 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482614 1072 #uuid "f598b188-bddf-5e21-881f-0c355a5c9889" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482614 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Line $\\text{L5}$ gives\n\n$$\n({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c.\n$$\n\nSince $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}={I}_{n}$, the right-hand side simplifies to $c$, so\n\n$$\nc=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nSimplify once, then apply $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}$, to obtain\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L6}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by the fact that $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L6}$, and $\\text{L7}.$ From $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c.\n$$\n\nUsing $\\text{L6}$, we substitute $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})$ to obtain\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L7}.$ Thus, $\\text{L7}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L6}$ by $\\text{substituting }c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\text{ into }x(t)={e}^{At}c.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L7}$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Since $At$ and $-A{t}_{0}$ commute, we can combine the exponentials:\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into line $\\text{L7}$ gives line $\\text{L8}.$ Thus, line $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by $\\text{the fact that }{e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}\\text{ when }X\\text{ and }Y\\text{ commute}.$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482393 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482615 1069 "q-162153" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482615 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482615 1071 "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y+3z\\le 2,\\,1\\le {z}^{3}\\le 8\\}.\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482615 1072 #uuid "f69775da-3c2e-512a-8912-f342f7d0a00f" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482615 1074 "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y+3z\\le 2,\\,1\\le {z}^{3}\\le 8,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=y+3z,\\,w={z}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 8\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (3{z}^{2})-3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{w}^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3{w}^{2/3}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3{w}^{2/3}}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{8}{w}^{-2/3}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{8}{w}^{-2/3}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[u]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [3{w}^{1/3}]_{w=1}^{w=8} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot ({8}^{1/3}-{1}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482616 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482447 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482616 1069 "q-162170" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482616 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482616 1071 "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le {y}^{2}-x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le e\\}.\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482616 1072 #uuid "fd1760e0-c4d7-5ae6-945d-3f3e267b54eb" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482616 1074 "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le {y}^{2}-x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le e,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={y}^{2}-x,\\,v=y,\\,w=z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1,\\,1\\le w\\le e\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{e}1\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{e}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=-1}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=e} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (1-(-1))\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =2(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482429 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482617 1069 "q-127090" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482617 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482617 1071 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{96}{595}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-2), & -1\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482617 1072 #uuid "fed1e82d-9029-565f-bbd7-a48e9974d84e" 13194139535226 17592186045417 true] [17592186482617 1074 "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-1\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2}$ is $f(x)=-\\frac{96}{595}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-2).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{96}{595}(4{x}^{3}-4x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{384}{595}x({x}^{2}-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{384}{595}x({x}^{2}-1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[-1,\\frac{3}{2}],$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=\\pm 1.$ So, these are critical points as well, but $-1$ is included above.\n\nNow, we